#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681} :activity/id #uuid "84d7f054-fed4-524c-b241-f09c7fd42e5e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120 :topic/id #uuid "dc267e07-dc7a-5013-bcb3-616951f01683" :topic/math-academy-id 149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060940 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" :tutorial/id #uuid "7aa753a7-1450-53dd-8e61-71e07952cecb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3df97c2e-febb-5f72-be71-e1d739dbbd92" :step/math-academy-id 17183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059580 :question/id #uuid "836f2f1b-5dd4-5dba-81cc-eb9ffe7db8d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4693"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1afe82-86ba-5a15-9037-74717692ecb2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388062}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To a Coefficients Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "46eeafc2-7d22-58a7-bc12-127e3c86f554" :step/math-academy-id 17184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059581 :question/id #uuid "2ff0a6f5-f3b2-5b39-8b69-1a0c969548cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4694"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c27a3f3c-77c7-55e9-8714-1f24c4a48063" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592